result:
Fixed. The bug was in how tokio built the abstract address:

- `tokio/src/net/unix/listener.rs:83` (`UnixListener::bind`)
- `tokio/src/net/unix/stream.rs:79` (`UnixStream::connect`)

Both detected a leading NUL in the path and then passed the *whole* byte slice — NUL included — to `SocketAddr::from_abstract_name`. That constructor prepends the NUL itself, so `"\0aaa"` was registered as the abstract name `"\0aaa"` (sun_path `\0\0aaa`) instead of `"aaa"`. Bind and connect agreed with each other, so connections worked, but the resulting address never matched the requested name — `as_abstract_name()` returned `b"\0aaa"`.

The fix strips the marker before handing the name over:

```rust
let os_str_bytes = path.as_ref().as_os_str().as_bytes();
if let Some(name) = os_str_bytes.strip_prefix(b"\0") {
    // `from_abstract_name` prepends the leading null byte itself, so
    // it must not be included in the name passed to it.
    StdSocketAddr::from_abstract_name(name)?
} else {
    StdSocketAddr::from_pathname(path)?
}
```

Verification (workspace root `Cargo.toml` was temporarily trimmed to the published crates so the offline build could resolve — it's restored, working tree contains only the two source edits):

- `uds_stream::abstract_socket_name` passes with the fix; reverting the two files reproduces the failure `left: [0, 97, 97], right: [97, 97, 97]`.
- Full `uds_stream` (8 tests), plus `uds_datagram`, `uds_split`, `uds_socket` all pass.

`cargo clippy` isn't installed in this environment, so no lint pass was run.
